{
  "paraId": "69722920",
  "style": "",
  "text": "At the cutoff date (2025 September 30), the median follow-up for all patients was 24.3 months (interquartile range 6.6 to 44.4 months). In our study, the 1-year PFS rate was 54.0%, exceeding the prespecified target. The 1.5-year and 2-year PFS rates were 42.2% and 28.6%, respectively, with a median PFS of 16.1 months (95% CI 11.4 to 19.2 months; Fig. 1G). The 1-year and 2-year OS rates were 83.1% and 57.7%, respectively, and the median OS was 29.3 months (95% CI 21.9 months to not reached; Fig. 1H). The ORR and DCR were 69.9% (58/83; 95% CI 58.8% to 79.5%) and 91.6% (76/83; 95% CI 83.4% to 96.5%), respectively (Fig. 2A and B). A total of 14 patients (16.9%) were downstaged to stage III and received hypofractionated radiotherapy. Among the remaining 69 patients, median PFS was 16.8 months (95% CI 11.3 to 19.2 months), and median OS was 24.0 months (95% CI 21.2 to 25.4 months). No statistically significant differences in PFS (P = 0.57; Fig. S1A) or OS (P = 0.45; Fig. S1B) were observed between the 14 patients who were downstaged to stage III and the remaining 69 patients. A total of 32 patients (38.6%) had distant metastasis beyond radiotherapy lesions, and 9 patients (10.8%) experienced both radiotherapy lesion progression and distant metastasis. Additionally, 20 patients (24.1%) experienced progression of LDRT lesions only, whereas no SABR lesions progressed (Table S6)."
}

